            <ArticleTitle>Poststroke shoulder pain: its relationship to motor impairment, activity limitation, and quality of life.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>298-301</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the relationship between poststroke shoulder pain, upper-limb motor impairment, activity limitation, and pain-related quality of life (QOL).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional, secondary analysis of baseline data from a multisite clinical trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient rehabilitation clinics of 7 academic medical centers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Volunteer sample of 61 chronic stroke survivors with poststroke shoulder pain and glenohumeral subluxation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Not applicable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">We measured poststroke shoulder pain with the Brief Pain Inventory question 12 (BPI 12), a self-reported 11-point numeric rating scale (NRS) that assesses &quot;worst pain&quot; in the last 7 days. Motor impairment was measured with the Fugl-Meyer Assessment (FMA). Activity limitation was measured with the Arm Motor Ability Test (AMAT) and the FIM instrument. Pain-related QOL was measured with BPI question 23, a self-reported 11-point NRS that assesses pain interference with general activity, mood, walking ability, normal work, interpersonal relationships, sleep, and enjoyment of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Stepwise regression analyses indicated that poststroke shoulder pain is associated with the BPI 23, but not with the FMA, FIM, or AMAT scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Poststroke shoulder pain is associated with reduced QOL, but not with motor impairment or activity limitation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
